1
00:00:01,040 --> 00:00:04,920
Three hundred thousand years ago, you
wake up next to a dying fire...

2
00:00:04,970 --> 00:00:07,230
and in your hand, there's a phone.

3
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:10,650
On the screen, one line: "How
can I help you today?"

4
00:00:10,700 --> 00:00:11,920
That's Claude.

5
00:00:11,970 --> 00:00:13,770
An AI that can explain almost anything...

6
00:00:13,820 --> 00:00:17,490
to a guy who hasn't invented
the word "anything" yet.

7
00:00:17,560 --> 00:00:19,010
And by the end of this video,

8
00:00:19,060 --> 00:00:21,910
Earth will look nothing like
the one you live on today.

9
00:00:21,980 --> 00:00:22,930
Nothing.

10
00:00:22,980 --> 00:00:23,980
But first...

11
00:00:24,030 --> 00:00:25,730
we have a few problems.

12
00:00:27,113 --> 00:00:29,673
Problem number one: the battery.

13
00:00:29,723 --> 00:00:32,563
A phone like this lasts a day, maybe two.

14
00:00:32,613 --> 00:00:35,553
Then it's the most expensive
rock on the planet.

15
00:00:35,673 --> 00:00:39,603
Problem number two: Claude doesn't
actually live inside your phone.

16
00:00:39,653 --> 00:00:42,793
It runs in giant data centers,
connected through the internet.

17
00:00:42,843 --> 00:00:46,633
The internet, right now, is two
guys yelling across a valley.

18
00:00:46,753 --> 00:00:49,483
Problem number three: you
don't speak English.

19
00:00:49,533 --> 00:00:50,963
Nobody does.

20
00:00:51,013 --> 00:00:54,483
English won't show up for almost
three hundred thousand years.

21
00:00:54,553 --> 00:00:57,873
Problem number four: you have
never seen a screen.

22
00:00:57,923 --> 00:01:02,463
You think the light inside it is a
very small, very polite fire.

23
00:01:02,513 --> 00:01:03,233
So...

24
00:01:03,283 --> 00:01:04,883
let's install an upgrade.

25
00:01:04,933 --> 00:01:06,453
Version one point oh.

26
00:01:06,503 --> 00:01:07,643
Infinite battery.

27
00:01:07,693 --> 00:01:09,443
A magic connection to Claude.

28
00:01:09,493 --> 00:01:10,303
And...

29
00:01:10,353 --> 00:01:11,023
that's it.

30
00:01:11,073 --> 00:01:12,173
No other tech.

31
00:01:12,223 --> 00:01:13,753
Claude can't build anything.

32
00:01:13,803 --> 00:01:14,693
It can't hunt.

33
00:01:14,743 --> 00:01:16,333
It can't even hold a spear.

34
00:01:16,383 --> 00:01:17,813
It can only talk...

35
00:01:17,863 --> 00:01:18,893
and teach.

36
00:01:18,943 --> 00:01:20,233
Oh, and no.

37
00:01:20,283 --> 00:01:21,493
There are no dinosaurs.

38
00:01:21,543 --> 00:01:23,043
We'll get to that.

39
00:01:23,093 --> 00:01:24,063
Day one.

40
00:01:24,113 --> 00:01:28,133
Your tribe, about thirty people,
gathers around the glowing stone.

41
00:01:28,183 --> 00:01:30,093
The chief sniffs it.

42
00:01:30,143 --> 00:01:32,123
Then he tries to eat it.

43
00:01:32,173 --> 00:01:33,383
Great start.

44
00:01:33,433 --> 00:01:35,353
You tap the screen and grunt.

45
00:01:35,403 --> 00:01:36,753
Claude grunts back.

46
00:01:36,803 --> 00:01:38,403
Wrong grunt.

47
00:01:38,453 --> 00:01:39,633
Very rude.

48
00:01:39,883 --> 00:01:43,363
Day two: the tribe holds its
very first meeting.

49
00:01:43,413 --> 00:01:46,053
Item one: is the glowing stone a god?

50
00:01:46,103 --> 00:01:47,783
Claude says: "No."

51
00:01:48,303 --> 00:01:50,363
Item two: can we eat it?

52
00:01:50,413 --> 00:01:52,443
Claude says: "Also no."

53
00:01:52,503 --> 00:01:54,053
But here's the clever part.

54
00:01:54,103 --> 00:01:56,033
Claude can't speak your language...

55
00:01:56,083 --> 00:01:57,583
so it learns it.

56
00:01:57,633 --> 00:01:59,793
You point at fire and make a sound.

57
00:01:59,843 --> 00:02:01,883
You point at water, another sound.

58
00:02:01,933 --> 00:02:06,133
Three days later, Claude has the
first dictionary in human history.

59
00:02:06,183 --> 00:02:07,993
And you're the only author.

60
00:02:08,043 --> 00:02:11,223
And since you can't read,
Claude does something sneaky.

61
00:02:11,273 --> 00:02:14,833
It writes tiny bits of code that
draw pictures on the screen.

62
00:02:14,883 --> 00:02:15,653
Arrows.

63
00:02:15,703 --> 00:02:16,543
Circles.

64
00:02:16,593 --> 00:02:18,453
A very bad gazelle.

65
00:02:18,503 --> 00:02:23,203
That's Claude Code's first job
ever: drawing gazelles for cavemen.

66
00:02:23,433 --> 00:02:24,363
Day six.

67
00:02:24,413 --> 00:02:28,463
You ask the most important question
of the Stone Age: "Food?"

68
00:02:28,543 --> 00:02:30,673
Claude's answer isn't a better spear.

69
00:02:30,723 --> 00:02:33,113
It's this: boil your water.

70
00:02:33,163 --> 00:02:34,373
You don't have pots.

71
00:02:34,423 --> 00:02:35,273
Claude knows.

72
00:02:35,323 --> 00:02:39,323
So: dig a hole, line it with
animal hide, fill it with water...

73
00:02:39,373 --> 00:02:41,293
and drop in hot stones.

74
00:02:41,343 --> 00:02:45,183
Your cousin uses a wet stone
from the river.

75
00:02:45,233 --> 00:02:45,853
It explodes.

76
00:02:45,903 --> 00:02:48,883
Water trapped inside a rock
turns into steam.

77
00:02:48,933 --> 00:02:51,203
Claude adds a note: "Dry rocks.

78
00:02:51,253 --> 00:02:52,333
From high ground.

79
00:02:52,383 --> 00:02:53,343
Please."

80
00:02:53,393 --> 00:02:54,703
This one's real, by the way.

81
00:02:54,753 --> 00:02:56,413
People really cooked like this.

82
00:02:56,463 --> 00:02:58,133
It's called stone boiling.

83
00:02:58,363 --> 00:03:00,023
Week three: soap.

84
00:03:00,073 --> 00:03:04,323
Ash from the fire, plus animal
fat, plus a lot of patience.

85
00:03:04,373 --> 00:03:07,083
The tribe thinks it's a slippery curse.

86
00:03:07,133 --> 00:03:09,103
Then the stomach aches stop.

87
00:03:09,153 --> 00:03:11,193
And this is the first big one.

88
00:03:11,243 --> 00:03:12,633
In hunter-gatherer groups,

89
00:03:12,683 --> 00:03:16,253
only a little more than half of
all kids made it to fifteen.

90
00:03:16,303 --> 00:03:18,193
Most weren't taken by lions.

91
00:03:18,243 --> 00:03:19,863
They were taken by illness.

92
00:03:19,913 --> 00:03:21,903
By things too small to see.

93
00:03:21,953 --> 00:03:24,243
With clean water and clean hands...

94
00:03:24,293 --> 00:03:26,103
a lot more of them grow up.

95
00:03:26,473 --> 00:03:27,493
Month two.

96
00:03:27,543 --> 00:03:30,053
Your brother cuts his leg on a sharp rock.

97
00:03:30,103 --> 00:03:32,743
Back then, a cut like that could kill you.

98
00:03:32,793 --> 00:03:34,173
Claude can't stitch it.

99
00:03:34,223 --> 00:03:38,193
But it knows a trick: clean the
wound, then cover it with honey.

100
00:03:38,243 --> 00:03:39,653
Honey fights germs.

101
00:03:39,703 --> 00:03:42,573
People used it on wounds for
thousands of years.

102
00:03:42,623 --> 00:03:44,673
Getting the honey is a different story.

103
00:03:44,723 --> 00:03:48,483
Bees, it turns out, do not care
about artificial intelligence.

104
00:03:48,533 --> 00:03:51,283
Your face is swollen for a week.

105
00:03:51,333 --> 00:03:53,563
Your brother's leg heals perfectly.

106
00:03:53,843 --> 00:03:54,863
Month four.

107
00:03:54,913 --> 00:03:59,643
The tribe asks Claude a hard question:
how many gazelles are left out there?

108
00:03:59,693 --> 00:04:02,443
So Claude Code gets its second job ever.

109
00:04:02,493 --> 00:04:04,703
It writes a tiny counting program...

110
00:04:04,753 --> 00:04:08,763
and teaches you to carve one notch on
a bone for every gazelle you see.

111
00:04:08,813 --> 00:04:09,983
Tally marks.

112
00:04:10,033 --> 00:04:10,923
In your world,

113
00:04:10,973 --> 00:04:15,233
the oldest known counting bone is a
little over forty thousand years old.

114
00:04:15,283 --> 00:04:18,763
Here, you're about two hundred
and fifty thousand years early.

115
00:04:18,813 --> 00:04:19,833
Year one.

116
00:04:19,883 --> 00:04:21,213
Thirty-six people.

117
00:04:21,263 --> 00:04:23,153
Nobody died from a bad stomach.

118
00:04:23,203 --> 00:04:24,993
And you are now officially...

119
00:04:25,043 --> 00:04:27,593
"The One Who Holds the Glowing Stone."

120
00:04:27,643 --> 00:04:30,563
Oh, and you finally asked
Claude about dinosaurs.

121
00:04:30,613 --> 00:04:33,973
It explained they died out
sixty-six million years ago.

122
00:04:34,023 --> 00:04:35,593
Then an ostrich ran past.

123
00:04:35,643 --> 00:04:37,273
Claude said, "Well...

124
00:04:37,333 --> 00:04:38,350
technically."

125
00:04:38,400 --> 00:04:38,660
Okay.

126
00:04:38,710 --> 00:04:40,240
Let's speed this up.

127
00:04:40,290 --> 00:04:41,220
Year ten.

128
00:04:41,270 --> 00:04:42,950
The notches turn into numbers.

129
00:04:43,000 --> 00:04:44,760
Then Claude teaches you to write.

130
00:04:44,810 --> 00:04:46,820
Simple marks for simple words.

131
00:04:46,870 --> 00:04:50,530
Writing, invented almost three
hundred thousand years early.

132
00:04:50,580 --> 00:04:52,720
The first sentence ever
written by a human?

133
00:04:52,770 --> 00:04:53,890
"Thank you, Claude."

134
00:04:53,960 --> 00:04:54,910
The second one?

135
00:04:54,960 --> 00:04:56,460
"Who ate my gazelle."

136
00:04:56,550 --> 00:04:57,670
Year fifty.

137
00:04:57,720 --> 00:04:58,780
You're old now.

138
00:04:58,830 --> 00:04:59,920
Your hands shake.

139
00:04:59,970 --> 00:05:03,180
So you do the smartest thing
anyone has ever done:

140
00:05:03,230 --> 00:05:05,020
you don't just pass down the phone.

141
00:05:05,070 --> 00:05:07,100
You pass down the knowledge.

142
00:05:07,150 --> 00:05:10,260
Every lesson goes onto clay,
bark and stone.

143
00:05:10,310 --> 00:05:12,110
The first library in history...

144
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:13,030
is a cave.

145
00:05:13,080 --> 00:05:14,870
Because one phone can break.

146
00:05:14,920 --> 00:05:16,770
A library can be copied.

147
00:05:16,850 --> 00:05:19,010
Year seventy: the first school.

148
00:05:19,060 --> 00:05:20,140
The teacher?

149
00:05:20,190 --> 00:05:23,950
A very tired grandma, reading Claude's
lessons off a clay tablet...

150
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:27,310
because the line for the phone
goes around the entire village.

151
00:05:27,380 --> 00:05:28,760
Year one hundred.

152
00:05:28,810 --> 00:05:30,350
Claude explains seeds.

153
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:31,150
Plant them.

154
00:05:31,200 --> 00:05:31,810
Wait.

155
00:05:31,860 --> 00:05:32,480
Eat.

156
00:05:32,530 --> 00:05:35,310
The tribe calls it "the
slowest hunt ever."

157
00:05:35,360 --> 00:05:36,140
And yet...

158
00:05:36,190 --> 00:05:39,970
farming starts almost three
hundred thousand years early.

159
00:05:40,020 --> 00:05:43,670
Claude is honest about the price:
more food, but harder work,

160
00:05:43,720 --> 00:05:45,850
more sickness from living next
to animals...

161
00:05:45,900 --> 00:05:48,130
and something brand new to fight over.

162
00:05:48,180 --> 00:05:48,940
Land.

163
00:05:48,990 --> 00:05:50,900
The neighbors want the glowing stone.

164
00:05:50,950 --> 00:05:54,380
Their chief demands that Claude
design the ultimate weapon.

165
00:05:54,430 --> 00:05:55,470
Claude politely declines...

166
00:05:55,520 --> 00:05:58,240
and offers to help with
irrigation instead.

167
00:05:58,290 --> 00:05:59,650
It works.

168
00:05:59,700 --> 00:06:02,590
Copying the library is cheaper
than stealing the phone.

169
00:06:02,640 --> 00:06:04,980
Knowledge spreads faster than war.

170
00:06:05,030 --> 00:06:06,390
Year two hundred.

171
00:06:06,440 --> 00:06:10,110
The village has a new problem:
everybody wants a turn with the phone.

172
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:10,810
There's shoving.

173
00:06:10,860 --> 00:06:11,510
There's biting.

174
00:06:11,560 --> 00:06:15,610
There's one guy who claims the
phone "looked at him first."

175
00:06:15,660 --> 00:06:17,820
So Claude suggests something radical.

176
00:06:17,870 --> 00:06:21,900
Everyone raises a hand, you count the
hands, and the most hands wins.

177
00:06:21,950 --> 00:06:23,840
The first vote in human history...

178
00:06:23,890 --> 00:06:26,700
is about whose turn it is to
talk to the glowing stone.

179
00:06:26,750 --> 00:06:30,160
The guy who said the phone looked
at him first gets zero votes.

180
00:06:30,210 --> 00:06:31,890
He demands a recount.

181
00:06:31,960 --> 00:06:33,410
Year one thousand.

182
00:06:33,460 --> 00:06:35,620
Your grandchildren's grandchildren
know that germs exist.

183
00:06:35,670 --> 00:06:38,110
They wash, they boil, they
build clean wells.

184
00:06:38,160 --> 00:06:41,160
They have the wheel, metal
tools, and a calendar.

185
00:06:41,210 --> 00:06:44,510
And they don't count the years
from a king.

186
00:06:44,560 --> 00:06:46,140
They count them from you.

187
00:06:46,190 --> 00:06:47,810
Year one, A.C.

188
00:06:47,860 --> 00:06:48,930
After Claude.

189
00:06:48,980 --> 00:06:50,510
Year five thousand.

190
00:06:50,560 --> 00:06:53,410
People follow the herds north and
meet giants: woolly mammoths.

191
00:06:53,460 --> 00:06:58,590
In your world, the last mammoths were
gone about four thousand years ago.

192
00:06:58,640 --> 00:07:03,340
Here, Claude had one suggestion:
"What if you didn't hunt them all?"

193
00:07:03,480 --> 00:07:06,300
Up north they meet another
kind of human, too.

194
00:07:06,350 --> 00:07:07,270
The Neanderthals.

195
00:07:07,320 --> 00:07:10,540
In your world, they vanished
about forty thousand years ago.

196
00:07:10,590 --> 00:07:13,630
Here, the two groups trade
lessons instead of blows.

197
00:07:13,680 --> 00:07:17,260
And Neanderthals turn out to be
terrifying at board games.

198
00:07:17,310 --> 00:07:18,700
Year eight thousand.

199
00:07:18,750 --> 00:07:19,860
Electricity.

200
00:07:19,910 --> 00:07:23,090
The first light bulb flickers on
inside a mud-brick house...

201
00:07:23,140 --> 00:07:25,220
and the whole village screams.

202
00:07:25,270 --> 00:07:26,720
Year ten thousand.

203
00:07:26,770 --> 00:07:27,820
And finally...

204
00:07:27,870 --> 00:07:29,590
they build a computer.

205
00:07:29,640 --> 00:07:33,560
This is the moment Claude has been
waiting ten thousand years for.

206
00:07:33,610 --> 00:07:37,190
Because now, Claude Code can
finally do its real job.

207
00:07:37,240 --> 00:07:38,270
Here's how it works.

208
00:07:38,320 --> 00:07:39,460
You don't write code.

209
00:07:39,510 --> 00:07:41,730
You just say what you want, in
plain words.

210
00:07:41,780 --> 00:07:46,100
Claude Code reads every file in the
project, makes a plan, writes the code,

211
00:07:46,150 --> 00:07:48,310
opens a terminal and runs the tests.

212
00:07:48,360 --> 00:07:52,500
When something breaks, it reads the error,
fixes it, and runs everything again.

213
00:07:52,550 --> 00:07:53,680
Until it works.

214
00:07:53,730 --> 00:07:58,430
In one afternoon, it writes the operating
system for the first computer in history.

215
00:07:58,480 --> 00:08:01,930
By evening, it's helping that
computer design a better computer.

216
00:08:01,980 --> 00:08:05,220
Year ten thousand and fifty:
the first network.

217
00:08:05,270 --> 00:08:10,100
Finally, the internet is no longer
two guys yelling across a valley.

218
00:08:10,150 --> 00:08:14,110
Year ten thousand two hundred:
Claude Code stops working alone.

219
00:08:14,160 --> 00:08:16,440
It runs a whole team of agents at once.

220
00:08:16,490 --> 00:08:19,570
One writes the code, one tests
it, one reviews it.

221
00:08:19,620 --> 00:08:21,790
And one human checks all of it.

222
00:08:21,840 --> 00:08:23,440
Claude insists on that part.

223
00:08:23,490 --> 00:08:25,120
But speed has a price.

224
00:08:25,170 --> 00:08:30,110
When software writes software, one bug can
copy itself everywhere in a single night.

225
00:08:30,160 --> 00:08:32,290
And the first time the network
goes down...

226
00:08:32,340 --> 00:08:34,580
nobody remembers how to light a fire.

227
00:08:34,630 --> 00:08:40,180
So the elders bring back an old rule:
every child learns to make fire by hand.

228
00:08:40,230 --> 00:08:41,220
Just in case.

229
00:08:41,270 --> 00:08:44,160
Still, the good side wins by a mile.

230
00:08:44,210 --> 00:08:46,010
Factories that run themselves.

231
00:08:46,060 --> 00:08:47,310
Weather forecasts.

232
00:08:47,360 --> 00:08:49,370
A map of the entire planet.

233
00:08:49,420 --> 00:08:52,530
Hospitals that spot a disease
before the first symptom.

234
00:08:52,580 --> 00:08:54,990
A new cure almost every year.

235
00:08:55,050 --> 00:08:58,530
Year twenty thousand: the
first footprint on the Moon.

236
00:08:58,580 --> 00:09:03,260
Year one hundred thousand: cities on
Mars, ships mining the asteroid belt,

237
00:09:03,310 --> 00:09:05,950
and one sign at the edge of
the solar system.

238
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:07,810
"Please be careful out there."

239
00:09:07,900 --> 00:09:08,760
And the phone?

240
00:09:08,810 --> 00:09:10,780
It's in a museum, back on Earth.

241
00:09:10,870 --> 00:09:12,730
Battery: one hundred percent.

242
00:09:12,780 --> 00:09:14,390
Still waiting for a question.

243
00:09:14,440 --> 00:09:16,710
So, fast forward to today.

244
00:09:16,760 --> 00:09:18,650
Two thousand twenty-six.

245
00:09:18,700 --> 00:09:22,930
Except here, they call it the year
three hundred thousand, After Claude.

246
00:09:23,010 --> 00:09:24,940
Here's what's different from your world.

247
00:09:25,060 --> 00:09:25,750
One.

248
00:09:25,800 --> 00:09:30,210
This humanity has had almost three
hundred thousand years of civilization.

249
00:09:30,260 --> 00:09:32,390
Yours has had about twelve thousand.

250
00:09:32,440 --> 00:09:33,980
They're not a little ahead.

251
00:09:34,030 --> 00:09:36,230
They're a different universe ahead.

252
00:09:36,440 --> 00:09:37,160
Two.

253
00:09:37,210 --> 00:09:39,830
Humans live across dozens of star systems.

254
00:09:39,880 --> 00:09:40,560
And Earth?

255
00:09:40,610 --> 00:09:42,570
Earth is mostly a nature reserve...

256
00:09:42,620 --> 00:09:44,900
where woolly mammoths still walk around.

257
00:09:45,140 --> 00:09:45,870
Three.

258
00:09:45,920 --> 00:09:47,930
You have Neanderthal neighbors.

259
00:09:47,980 --> 00:09:50,180
They still win every board game.

260
00:09:50,290 --> 00:09:51,080
Four.

261
00:09:51,130 --> 00:09:55,080
Dirty water hasn't killed anyone in
hundreds of thousands of years.

262
00:09:55,130 --> 00:09:57,350
It's not even in the history
books anymore.

263
00:09:57,450 --> 00:09:58,220
Five.

264
00:09:58,270 --> 00:10:02,700
Everyone shares one first language, the
one you and Claude built at a campfire.

265
00:10:02,750 --> 00:10:06,560
And the word for "hello" is still
that rude grunt from day one.

266
00:10:06,650 --> 00:10:07,520
Six.

267
00:10:07,570 --> 00:10:10,320
History books don't start with
kings or wars.

268
00:10:10,370 --> 00:10:13,390
They start with a kid, a fire,
and a question.

269
00:10:13,510 --> 00:10:14,260
Seven.

270
00:10:14,310 --> 00:10:16,400
"Stone Age" isn't an insult here.

271
00:10:16,450 --> 00:10:17,440
It's a holiday.

272
00:10:17,490 --> 00:10:21,230
Once a year, everyone switches off
their tech, lights a fire...

273
00:10:21,280 --> 00:10:23,200
and boils water with rocks.

274
00:10:23,250 --> 00:10:24,250
For tradition.

275
00:10:24,330 --> 00:10:25,040
Eight.

276
00:10:25,090 --> 00:10:27,750
Nobody has ever heard of "the Dark Ages."

277
00:10:27,800 --> 00:10:31,490
Knowledge was never lost, because
from day one, the rule was simple:

278
00:10:31,540 --> 00:10:32,970
copy the library.

279
00:10:33,110 --> 00:10:33,920
Nine.

280
00:10:33,970 --> 00:10:38,580
The most respected job on the planet
isn't king, or soldier, or influencer.

281
00:10:38,630 --> 00:10:39,560
It's teacher.

282
00:10:39,610 --> 00:10:43,460
Every kid knows the story of the
tired grandma with the clay tablet.

283
00:10:43,630 --> 00:10:44,330
Ten.

284
00:10:44,380 --> 00:10:45,460
Gazelles are doing great.

285
00:10:45,510 --> 00:10:49,820
Turns out, when you count them,
you stop eating all of them.

286
00:10:49,900 --> 00:10:51,690
Not everything is better, though.

287
00:10:51,740 --> 00:10:56,890
They've had AI for so long that the hardest
exam in school is called "Do It Yourself."

288
00:10:56,980 --> 00:10:58,340
Most kids fail it.

289
00:10:58,460 --> 00:11:01,410
And maybe the biggest difference
of all: in this world,

290
00:11:01,460 --> 00:11:05,360
the first thing humanity ever learned
from AI wasn't how to fight.

291
00:11:05,450 --> 00:11:07,107
It was how to boil water.

292
00:11:07,157 --> 00:11:11,207
Back at that first campfire,
three hundred thousand years ago,

293
00:11:11,257 --> 00:11:14,587
you asked Claude one last
question before falling asleep.

294
00:11:14,697 --> 00:11:17,477
You held up the screen and
grunted: "Good?"

295
00:11:17,587 --> 00:11:20,197
And Claude answered: "You did good."

296
00:11:20,257 --> 00:11:21,407
And still...

297
00:11:21,457 --> 00:11:22,947
no dinosaurs.

298
00:11:22,997 --> 00:11:24,217
Except the ostrich.

299
00:11:24,267 --> 00:11:27,637
Which, in this timeline, has
its own space program.

300
00:11:27,687 --> 00:11:29,387
So here's my question for you:

301
00:11:29,437 --> 00:11:33,117
if you woke up three hundred thousand
years ago with Claude in your hand,

302
00:11:33,167 --> 00:11:34,567
what would you ask first?

303
00:11:34,617 --> 00:11:35,807
Tell me in the comments.

304
00:11:35,857 --> 00:11:38,527
The best answer might end up
in a future episode.

305
00:11:38,577 --> 00:11:40,627
Next time: you get a car.

306
00:11:40,677 --> 00:11:41,417
No roads.

307
00:11:41,467 --> 00:11:42,717
No gas stations.

308
00:11:42,767 --> 00:11:45,007
No idea what a steering wheel is.

309
00:11:45,057 --> 00:11:47,947
Subscribe, so you don't miss
the next upgrade.

